\title{mediaPlanR: Reliable Cross-Media Reach and Frequency Planning}
\description{
Reproducible tools for cross-media reach, contact-frequency distributions,
target-audience metrics and budget-constrained reach optimization. The
package combines a validated media-plan data contract with the classical
reach and exposure-distribution models of the media-planning literature.
}
\section{Scope of the validation}{

The models can be applied to any mix of media, but the published evidence on
their accuracy comes mainly from magazine and Internet data. Their accuracy
for a particular combination of media and population should be checked
against observed data, for example with \code{\link[=evaluate_exposure_model]{evaluate_exposure_model()}}.

Three kinds of evidence must not be confused. \emph{Replication of a published
example or table}: in the maintainer's local tests, whose input tables are
not distributed, the package reproduces, within 0.01 percentage points, the
CBD columns of the 80 two-vehicle plans of Kim (2005) and Hong (1998), but
those sources do not print the duplications, which were reconstructed from
another model's published column; this is a consistency check between
columns, not an independent test. \emph{Fidelity to the printed algorithm}: the
functions follow the procedures described in the sources, and where the
printed numbers cannot be reproduced the difference is recorded in the
tests. \emph{Predictive validation}: agreement with independent observations of
reach and frequency. The package does not provide it and the replicated
tables do not supply it; use \code{\link[=evaluate_exposure_model]{evaluate_exposure_model()}} with your own
data. The provenance of each dataset is recorded in the installed file
\code{DATA-PROVENANCE.md} (see \code{system.file("DATA-PROVENANCE.md", package = "mediaPlanR")}).
}

\section{Notation and units}{

The same quantity has the same meaning in every function. Names follow the
sources of each model only where the literature fixes them.
\itemize{
\item \emph{Population} (number of people in the planning universe): \code{population}
everywhere. In \code{\link[=audience_metrics]{audience_metrics()}}, \code{gross_universe} is the whole
population and \code{target_universe} the people who belong to the target.
\item \emph{Audience} (people exposed by one insertion of a vehicle): \code{audience}
in \code{\link[=media_plan]{media_plan()}} and \code{audiences} (one value per vehicle) in the
\verb{calc_*()} functions, always in people. As a proportion of the
population it is \code{R1}.
\item \emph{Reach after the first and second insertion of the same vehicle}:
\code{R1} and \code{R2} (Kim, 2005, and the sources of each model), always as
proportions of the population, in \code{\link[=calc_beta_binomial]{calc_beta_binomial()}},
\code{\link[=calc_hofmans_accumulation]{calc_hofmans_accumulation()}}, \code{\link[=calibrate_bbd]{calibrate_bbd()}}, \code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}} and the
sequential models. \code{R2} is cumulative: the people exposed at least once
in two insertions. If the audience is known in people, divide it by the
population.
\item \emph{Number of insertions}: \code{insertions} (one value per vehicle) in all
functions; the formulas use \eqn{n_i} for a vehicle and \eqn{N} for the
total. The column of \code{vehicles_data} in \code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}} may also be named
\code{k}, Danaher's notation. The argument \code{k} of
\code{\link[=calc_agostini_duplication]{calc_agostini_duplication()}} is something else: Agostini's duplication
coefficient.
\item \emph{Duplication between two vehicles}: \code{duplication_matrix}, in people
(\code{\link[=calc_agostini_duplication]{calc_agostini_duplication()}}, \code{\link[=calc_hofmans_duplication]{calc_hofmans_duplication()}},
\code{\link[=calc_metheringham]{calc_metheringham()}}); \code{duplications}, as proportions of the
population (\code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}} and the sequential models). The different
names signal the different units.
\item \emph{Reach} (also net audience) counts each person once. \emph{Contacts}
(also impressions, or gross audience in the duplication models) count a
person once per insertion: with several insertions they are the sum of
each audience times its insertions, \eqn{I = \sum n_i A_i}; with one
insertion per vehicle they are the sum of the audiences, the \emph{gross
audience} of \code{\link[=calc_agostini_duplication]{calc_agostini_duplication()}} and \code{\link[=calc_hofmans_duplication]{calc_hofmans_duplication()}}.
The \code{gross_audience} argument of \code{\link[=audience_metrics]{audience_metrics()}} is something else:
the total audience of a channel, whatever the profile of its members.
\emph{Duplications} between vehicles are coincidences between pairs, so a
person present in three vehicles contributes three pairs, not three
people. Write the unit (people, contacts, pairs or proportion) before
forming a ratio. With these symbols, the average frequency is
\eqn{F = I / R} (contacts per person reached, defined only if \eqn{R > 0})
and, per person of the universe, the mean number of contacts is
\eqn{\mu = I / P}, so that \eqn{GRP = 100 \mu}.
\item \emph{Effective frequency}: \code{effective_frequency} in
\code{\link[=optimize_media_plan]{optimize_media_plan()}} and \code{frequency} in \code{\link[=calibrate_bbd]{calibrate_bbd()}}; effective
reach is the proportion exposed that many times or more.
\item \emph{Units}: every argument documents its unit. A \emph{proportion} is a
number between 0 and 1 (0.35 for 35\%, not 35); percentages appear in
result columns named \code{percent} (and \code{reach_percent}); counts of people
appear in arguments described as "in people" and in result columns named
\code{people}. There are three exceptions, and they are listed in the help
page of each function: \code{zero_contact_probability} in
\code{\link[=calc_beta_binomial]{calc_beta_binomial()}} is a percentage (0 to 100) despite its name;
\code{affinity_index} in \code{\link[=audience_metrics]{audience_metrics()}} has base 100 and can exceed 100;
and the rating points of \code{\link[=plan_metrics]{plan_metrics()}} (\code{grps}, \code{rating_points}) are
points per 100 people of the universe, which can also exceed 100 when
contacts exceed the population.
\item \emph{Errors of evaluation}: the average percentage errors of
\code{\link[=evaluate_exposure_model]{evaluate_exposure_model()}} (\code{kim_aer}, \code{kim_ape}) are relative errors
returned as proportions (multiply by 100 for percent); absolute errors of
reach are differences between proportions (multiply by 100 for
percentage points, so 0.002 of probability is 0.2 percentage points).
}
}

\section{Assumptions of the models}{

Each model rests on assumptions about four things. They are the first thing
to check when choosing a model.
\itemize{
\item \emph{Individuals}: homogeneous (every person has the same probability of
being exposed to an insertion of a vehicle) or heterogeneous
(probabilities differ between people, as between loyal and occasional
readers). Heterogeneity makes repeated exposures concentrate in the same
people, so reach grows more slowly with the number of insertions.
\code{\link[=calc_binomial]{calc_binomial()}} and \code{\link[=calc_sainsbury]{calc_sainsbury()}} assume homogeneous individuals;
the Beta-Binomial family (\code{\link[=calc_beta_binomial]{calc_beta_binomial()}}, \code{\link[=calc_metheringham]{calc_metheringham()}},
\code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}}, \code{\link[=calc_csd]{calc_csd()}}, \code{\link[=calc_msad]{calc_msad()}}, \code{\link[=calc_cbd]{calc_cbd()}},
\code{\link[=calc_mbd]{calc_mbd()}}) lets the probability vary between people.
\item \emph{Vehicles}: homogeneous (every vehicle has the plan's average
audience) or heterogeneous (each has its own audience).
\code{\link[=calc_binomial]{calc_binomial()}} and \code{\link[=calc_metheringham]{calc_metheringham()}} treat vehicles as
homogeneous; \code{\link[=calc_sainsbury]{calc_sainsbury()}} and the multivariate models keep each
vehicle's audience.
\item \emph{Duplication between vehicles}: random (people choose vehicles
independently, so the audience shared by two vehicles is the product of
their audiences) or observed (taken from data). \code{\link[=calc_binomial]{calc_binomial()}} and
\code{\link[=calc_sainsbury]{calc_sainsbury()}} assume random duplication; the other models with
several vehicles use observed duplication.
\item \emph{Accumulation in the same vehicle}: random (a repeat insertion in
a vehicle is as independent as an insertion in another vehicle) or
observed through the reach after two insertions (\code{R2}), which captures
that those who saw the first insertion tend to see the next.
\code{\link[=calc_binomial]{calc_binomial()}} and \code{\link[=calc_sainsbury]{calc_sainsbury()}} assume random accumulation; the
models for several insertions in a vehicle use the observed
accumulation: \code{R2} or, in \code{\link[=calc_metheringham]{calc_metheringham()}}, the audience
duplicated between two insertions of the same vehicle.
}
All the models also assume that exposure probabilities are stationary
(they do not change over the period of the plan). Each help page states the
assumptions of its model.
}

\section{Planning workflow}{

\code{\link[=media_plan]{media_plan()}} creates a validated plan, \code{\link[=plan_metrics]{plan_metrics()}} and
\code{\link[=audience_metrics]{audience_metrics()}} compute its metrics, \code{\link[=estimate_reach]{estimate_reach()}} and
\code{\link[=compare_reach_models]{compare_reach_models()}} estimate reach and the exposure distribution, and
\code{\link[=optimize_media_plan]{optimize_media_plan()}} allocates insertions under a budget.
}

\section{Suggested study path}{

The help pages of a reference manual are in alphabetical order, which
assumes prior knowledge. For sequential study, read in this order: (1) the
sections "Notation and units" and "Assumptions of the models" above, to fix
what is counted (people, contacts, pairs) and what each model assumes; (2) the
models of independence, \code{\link[=calc_sainsbury]{calc_sainsbury()}} and \code{\link[=calc_binomial]{calc_binomial()}}, and
\code{\link[=estimate_reach]{estimate_reach()}}; (3) the ad hoc duplication formulas,
\code{\link[=calc_agostini_duplication]{calc_agostini_duplication()}} and \code{\link[=calc_hofmans_duplication]{calc_hofmans_duplication()}}; (4) the
accumulation of one vehicle, \code{\link[=calc_beta_binomial]{calc_beta_binomial()}} and
\code{\link[=calc_hofmans_accumulation]{calc_hofmans_accumulation()}}, with \code{\link[=fit_bbd_to_reach]{fit_bbd_to_reach()}} and
\code{\link[=calibrate_bbd]{calibrate_bbd()}}; (5) the multivariate models, \code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}}, \code{\link[=calc_csd]{calc_csd()}},
\code{\link[=calc_msad]{calc_msad()}}, \code{\link[=calc_cbd]{calc_cbd()}} and \code{\link[=calc_mbd]{calc_mbd()}}, and the section "Domain of
validity" of \code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}}; (6) the count approximation
\code{\link[=nbd_exposure_distribution]{nbd_exposure_distribution()}}; (7) evaluation, \code{\link[=evaluate_exposure_model]{evaluate_exposure_model()}};
and (8) optimization, \code{\link[=optimize_media_plan]{optimize_media_plan()}}. \code{vignette("mediaPlanR-intro")}
gives worked examples of the planning workflow and of the models; it is
the place for a longer teaching development, which the reference pages do not
attempt.
}

\section{Reach and exposure-distribution models}{

Plans with one or more insertions in each of several vehicles, with random
duplication and accumulation: \code{\link[=calc_sainsbury]{calc_sainsbury()}}, \code{\link[=calc_binomial]{calc_binomial()}}.
One vehicle with several insertions (accumulation models):
\code{\link[=calc_beta_binomial]{calc_beta_binomial()}}, \code{\link[=calc_hofmans_accumulation]{calc_hofmans_accumulation()}}. Several vehicles with one insertion each:
\code{\link[=calc_agostini_duplication]{calc_agostini_duplication()}}, \code{\link[=calc_hofmans_duplication]{calc_hofmans_duplication()}}. Several
vehicles with several insertions: \code{\link[=calc_metheringham]{calc_metheringham()}}, \code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}},
\code{\link[=calc_csd]{calc_csd()}}, \code{\link[=calc_msad]{calc_msad()}}, \code{\link[=calc_cbd]{calc_cbd()}} and \code{\link[=calc_mbd]{calc_mbd()}}. Fitting and
validation: \code{\link[=fit_bbd_to_reach]{fit_bbd_to_reach()}}, \code{\link[=calibrate_bbd]{calibrate_bbd()}}, \code{\link[=fit_nbd_exposure]{fit_nbd_exposure()}},
\code{\link[=nbd_exposure_distribution]{nbd_exposure_distribution()}} and \code{\link[=evaluate_exposure_model]{evaluate_exposure_model()}}.
}

